@@ -189,6 +189,50 @@ Below is an example using the [`InputBase`](/material-ui/api/input-base/) compon
 
 🎨 If you are looking for inspiration, you can check [MUI Treasury's customization examples](https://mui-treasury.com/primitive/text-field).
 
+### Custom variants
+
+Beyond the built-in `outlined`, `filled` and `standard` variants, you can add your own with the `variants` key of the theme, as described in the [theme components](/material-ui/customization/theme-components/#variants) documentation.
+
+```js
+const theme = createTheme({
+  components: {
+    MuiTextField: {
+      variants: [
+        {
+          // `dashed` is an example value, it can be any name.
+          props: { variant: 'dashed' },
+          style: {
+            border: `2px dashed ${blue[500]}`,
+          },
+        },
+      ],
+    },
+  },
+});
+```
+
+If you're using TypeScript, you'll need to specify your new variant, using [module augmentation](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/declaration-merging.html#module-augmentation).
+
+<!-- Tested with packages/mui-material/test/typescript/moduleAugmentation/textFieldVariants.spec.tsx -->
+
+```tsx
+declare module '@mui/material/TextField' {
+  interface TextFieldPropsVariantOverrides {
+    dashed: true;
+  }
+}
+```
+
+The same interface exists on the components the text field composes—`FormControlPropsVariantOverrides`, `InputLabelPropsVariantOverrides`, `InputAdornmentPropsVariantOverrides`, `SelectPropsVariantOverrides` and `NativeSelectPropsVariantOverrides`—so augment those too if you use them directly.
+
+A custom variant has no input component of its own, so `TextField` renders the unstyled [`InputBase`](/material-ui/api/input-base/) for it, and types `slotProps.input` and `onChange` accordingly. Use the `input` slot to render a different one:
+
+```jsx
+<TextField variant="dashed" slots={{ input: OutlinedInput }} />
+```
+
+Note that a custom variant never renders a notched outline, as that behavior is specific to the built-in `outlined` variant.
+
 ## `useFormControl`
 
 For advanced customization use cases, a `useFormControl()` hook is exposed.
